## ZITADEL Authentication

Authentication is built into this template and runs in the TanStack Start
server runtime. The home page is public; `/dashboard` requires a signed-in
user. The app uses OpenID Connect Authorization Code with PKCE. The browser
receives only an opaque, HTTP-only session handle; the user's minimal identity
and OAuth access/refresh tokens stay in Redis for at most eight hours. Logout
removes the Redis session and attempts to revoke its refresh token before
redirecting through ZITADEL's end-session endpoint. Server code can call the
configured Axum API with a bearer token using `fetchAuthenticatedApi` from
`src/server/api.server.ts`. Tokens are never returned to browser components.
The flow follows ZITADEL's
[Authorization Code + PKCE guide](https://zitadel.com/docs/guides/integrate/login/oidc/login-users).

### Local setup

1. Start the local ZITADEL instance. In this repository, `apps/auth-server`
   provides it on `http://localhost:8081`; start it separately using its README.
   Start Redis separately, for example:

   ```bash
   docker run --name tanstack-redis -p 6379:6379 -d redis:7-alpine
   ```

2. In the ZITADEL Console, create a Web application using Authorization Code
   with PKCE, and enable the **Refresh Token** grant in the app's settings.
   Request `offline_access` and the project audience scope. Register these exact
   URLs:

   - Redirect URI: `http://localhost:3000/auth/callback`
   - Post-logout redirect URI: `http://localhost:3000/auth/logout/callback`

   Enable Development Mode to allow these local HTTP URLs. Copy the
   application's client ID and the containing ZITADEL project ID. The PKCE
   flow does not use a client secret.
3. Copy `.env.example` to `.env`, set `ZITADEL_CLIENT_ID` and
   `ZITADEL_PROJECT_ID`, confirm `REDIS_URL` and `API_BASE_URL`, and generate a
   session secret of at least 32 characters:

   ```bash
   cp .env.example .env
   openssl rand -base64 32
   ```

   Set `SESSION_SECRET` in `.env` to the generated value. The local defaults
   for `APP_URL` and `ZITADEL_ISSUER` are already configured in the example.
4. Run `bun run dev` and use **Log in** in the header. `/dashboard` makes a
   server-to-server `GET /api/me` call when `API_BASE_URL` is configured; it
   never forwards the browser's cookie to Axum.

To connect a generated project to an API, also generate and configure the
`templates/axum` template. Each Axum instance needs its own ZITADEL API app and
downloaded Private Key JWT key file, but APIs in the same ZITADEL project can
share the audience scope requested by this web app.

For production, use HTTPS for the app, issuer, and API, use a secured Redis
deployment, set the variables in the hosting environment, and register the
production callback and post-logout URLs in ZITADEL. This template requires a
server runtime; a static export cannot handle OIDC callbacks or sessions.

Login always ships with generated projects, whether or not demo routes are
included.

# Building For Production

To build this application for production:

```bash
bun run build
```

## Styling

This project uses [Tailwind CSS](https://tailwindcss.com/) for styling.

### Removing Tailwind CSS

If you prefer not to use Tailwind CSS:

1. Remove the demo pages in `src/routes/demo/`
2. Replace the Tailwind import in `src/styles.css` with your own styles
3. Remove `tailwindcss()` from the plugins array in `vite.config.ts`
4. Remove `@tailwindcss/vite` and `tailwindcss` from `package.json`

## Linting & Formatting

This project uses [Biome](https://biomejs.dev/) for linting and formatting. The following scripts are available:


```bash
bun run lint
bun run format
bun run check
bun run test
```


